(!) means you're externally affecting the process

n.b.: CTRL+ hotkeys are given in the POSIXy way where SHIFT is not a modifier and letters
are key chars instead of VKs; this is relevant if you have a non-US QWERTY keyboard layout

You also need to install libcc1.so on the system (gcc as a library).
compile code -- C code	compile & execute (!) C code; use set language cpp to switch to cpp
compile code filename	compile & execute (!) C code from filename
set compile-args	set the gcc/g++ compiler options
The C code is valid C code within the scope 

You can also compile a helper shared library (e.g. to pretty print stuff, or just to have symbols where you need them) with g++ -fPIC -shared + other relevant flags
This method doesn't require anything special from GDB. It relies on libc's implementation of dlopen. We're lucky libc is normally loaded in by gdb.
call (void*)__libc_dlopen_mode("./helper.so", 2) 				# or 0x102 in case libc changes in the future in weird and interesting ways)
call (return_type)__helperfunction(arg1, arg2)					# where __helperfunction is e.g. extern return_type __helperfunction(int, Object*)

Your helper functions can contain printers, or just link in a lot of application symbols. You can pretty much just dlopen almost any random thing and call stuff from it.

